    "body": "Currently the code doesn't handle the case where an invoice initiated from the CLI isn't paid. The output in the terminal still says \"Invoice Paid\" for cases where the mint doesn't support websockets and where it does, it hangs indefinitely.\n\n**Reproduction**\nBefore running the server:\n1. To reproduce this while testing in the cli via FakeWallet, you can turn off auto invoice payment by setting  `fakewallet_brr=False` in the settings file. ( cashu/core/settings.py ) under the FakeWalletSettings class.\n\n     This will remove the automatic payment of the invoice generated by the cli\n2. Run the server\n3. Run the command `cashu invoice 99` or any other amount\n    If the mint supports websockets, the code will hang in this code block ( in cashu/wallet/cli/cli.py ) while waiting for the invoice to be paid\n    ```python\n    if mint_supports_websockets:\n        while not paid:\n            await asyncio.sleep(0.1)\n    ```\n    If the mint doesn't support websockets, then the code polls the invoice state for 5 minutes after which this is executed\n    ```python\n    if not paid:\n        print(\"\\n\")\n        print(\n                  \"Invoice is not paid yet, stopping check. Use the command above to\"\n                  \" recheck after the invoice has been paid.\"\n                )\n    ```\n    But after this, it simply falls through to the end of the method and then \"Invoice Paid\" is printed despite the invoice not being paid\n    ```python\n    # close open subscriptions so we can exit\n        try:\n            subscription.close()\n        except Exception:\n            pass\n        print(\" Invoice paid.\")\n    \n        print(\"\")\n        await print_balance(ctx)\n        return\n    ```\n    It's also worth noting that the `mint_quote` object already has an `expiry` field but neither execution path utilizes it.\n\n\n**Test Gap**\n\nI noticed that the tests also don't have a pipeline to focusing on if an invoice is not paid. I have not yet thoroughly investigated the testing aspect of it, but the indefinite websocket hang is the type of thing that could cause some tests to hang. Highlighted in #952 \n\nMy suspicions are also led by the fact that I experienced a the same while running the tests and the test file that hung was `test_wallet_cli.py` - which tests the particular code that this issue is focused on.",
